// expect-exit: 0
//
// NOT A PROBE IN THE ORDINARY SENSE. This file exits 0 and that IS the
// finding, exactly as `probe06b_element_leak.npk` does -- and it is a SECOND,
// DIFFERENT way to orphan an element that no gate in this language notices.
//
// PROBE 12 -- OVERWRITING an occupied `Vec<T>` element discards the old one,
// and `v.items[i] = move(x)` does not drop it.
//
// ASKS: `0.0.4.md` §2's note names THREE entries that discard elements --
// `vec_free`, `vec_clear` and `vec_truncate` -- and says each needs an
// element-drop path for an owning `T`. Is that list complete?
//
// IT IS NOT. `vec_set` overwrites an element, which discards exactly one, and
// it is missing from that list, from `SAFETY.md` S-18b's statement of the same
// rule, and from TM-106. Measured 2026-09-05 at pin `0dfddac`, 2 000 000
// overwrites of one occupied `Vec<string>` slot:
//
//   overwritten in place, outgoing element orphaned   125 184 KiB   exit 0
//   outgoing element moved out into a dying scope       1 596 KiB   exit 0
//
// and, because a peak-RSS number is arguable and an exit code is not, the same
// pair under a shrinking address-space cap:
//
//   ulimit -v      131072   65536   32768    8192    3072    2048
//   probe12             0      92      92      92      92     127
//   probe12b            0       0       0       0       0     127
//
// 92 is `HeapOom`, the runtime's own arm. THE 2048 ROW IS NOT A RESULT: at
// that cap `/bin/true` also fails to exec ("failed to map segment from shared
// object"), so 127 is the machine's floor and not the program's. Read the
// 3072 row as the answer: the remedy half completes 2 000 000 iterations
// inside a 3 MiB address space and the leaking half does not.
//
// THE TWIN IS `probe12b_set_overwrite_drop.npk`, which differs in the ONE
// STATEMENT that moves the old element into a scoped local before writing.
// Read them side by side; the diff is the finding.
//
// RUN IT AS: /usr/bin/time -f "%e s  %M KiB" <binary>
// AND AS:    ( ulimit -v 65536 ; <binary> ) ; echo $?
//
// WHY IT IS NOT A COMPILER DEFECT, WHICH TOOK TWO CONTROLS TO ESTABLISH. The
// compiler's D-186 is titled "overwriting an owning field or managed element
// drops the old value", so the first reading was that this contradicts it.
// It does not:
//
//   CONTROL A -- the same 2 000 000 overwrites into a LOCAL BINDING
//                (`s = string_concat(...)`)                1 660 KiB, exit 0
//                under a 64 MiB cap                        exit 0
//   CONTROL B -- the same overwrites written DIRECTLY at the site, with no
//                function call anywhere            125 184 KiB, exit 92 capped
//
// A binding drops. A `wild T->` element does not, whether it is written
// through a generic function, through a concrete one, or inline. D-186's
// "managed element" is a MANAGED one; `Vec<T>.items` is `wild`, which is the
// MANUAL regime, and the manual regime does not drop for you -- which is the
// same sentence that makes `vec_free` the caller's job. So this is the `wild`
// qualifier behaving exactly as specified, and the obligation is the library's.
//
// THE BLAST RADIUS IS WIDER THAN `vec_set`. Any assignment to `v.items[i]`
// over an occupied slot discards an element: `vec_set`, and any future
// in-place update. `vec_push` is SAFE for a different reason -- it writes at
// `count`, which is past the last live element by construction -- and that
// distinction is worth keeping, because it is not visible in the two lines of
// code, which are identical.
//
// WHY BOTH HALVES ARE CONCRETE (`Vec<string>`) RATHER THAN GENERIC, WHICH IS
// ITSELF A FINDING. The first draft of the remedy half was a generic
// `vec_set<T>` that moved the outgoing element into a local. It does not
// compile: `npkc` accepts it at exit 0 and `llc` refuses the IR with
// `use of undefined value '@npk.vacant.1881'` -- O-N17,
// `defect/generic_element_move/`. So at an owning `T` a generic `vec_set<T>`
// TODAY either leaks (this file's shape) or does not link (the remedy's), and
// there is no third spelling. That HELD `vec_pop<T>`'s row, and it is why
// this pair is written at the instantiation, which is where `SAFETY.md` S-18b
// already puts element lifetime.
//
// CORRECTED 2026-09-05 AT PIN `aaffb87` (cycle 0.0.5, TM-136). The paragraph
// above is `0dfddac`'s and is left standing because it was true there. Two of
// its claims are not true here. **O-N17 IS FIXED**, so the generic remedy no
// longer fails at `llc`; and **"there is no third spelling" was wrong even
// about the two it names** -- at this pin a generic `vec_set<T>` that moves the
// outgoing element into a dying local compiles, links and runs, PROVIDED the
// slice is re-made after the move (`npkc` 0 with a 61 044 B `.ll`, `llc` 0,
// `ld.lld` 0, exit 0). Writing through the SAME `#wild_slice` binding the move
// came out of is `NITPICK-MOVE-001`, because moving out of an element
// invalidates the binding it was reached through.
//
// THIS PAIR IS UNCHANGED AND MEASURES WHAT IT ALWAYS DID -- the leak and its
// remedy at the instantiation, which is where `SAFETY.md` S-18b puts element
// lifetime and where TM-136 keeps it. The reason moved: not that the generic
// spelling cannot be written, but that `NITPICK-TYPE-046` does not fire inside
// a generic body, so nothing would catch the next one written without a
// `move` (`defect/generic_owning_copy/`).
mod:probe12_set_overwrite_leak;

struct:Vec<T> = { wild T->:items; int64:count; int64:cap; };

func:vec_init_string = Vec<string>(int64:cap) never fails {
    int64:n = cap;
    if (n < 1i64) { n = 1i64; }
    wild int8->:mem = alloc(n * #size_of<string>());
    pass Vec{ items: mem =>! wild string->, count: 0i64, cap: n };
};

// THE LINE UNDER TEST. The outgoing element is discarded and nothing drops it.
func:vec_set_string = NIL(Vec<string>->:v, int64:i, move string:x) never fails {
    v.items[i] = move(x);
    pass NIL;
};

func:vec_free_string = NIL(Vec<string>->:v) never fails {
    dalloc(v.items =>! wild int8->);
    v.count = 0i64;
    v.cap = 0i64;
    pass NIL;
};

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) {
    Vec<string>:b = raw vec_init_string(1i64);
    // 20 + 15 = 35 bytes on the managed heap, once, to occupy the slot.
    b.items[0i64] = string_concat("Australia/Lord_Howe_", "Europe/Kyiv_xyz");
    b.count = 1i64;
    int64:i = 0i64;
    while (i < 2000000i64) {
        // THE LEAK: the slot takes the new element and the old one is orphaned.
        drop vec_set_string(@b, 0i64, string_concat("Australia/Lord_Howe_", "Europe/Kyiv_xyz"));
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    // Empty the slot properly at the end, so the ONLY difference from the twin
    // is what happens inside the loop.
    string:last = move(b.items[0i64]);
    discard(string_byte_length(last));
    b.count = 0i64;
    drop vec_free_string(@b);
    // D-151 asserts no `wild` allocation is live -- and the block WAS paired,
    // so this exits 0. Two million managed bodies are still live and the gate
    // is silent about all of them. That is TM-106's mechanism, reached through
    // a door TM-106 did not name.
    exit 0i32;
};

func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (HeapBadRequest) { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)        { exit 92i32; },
        (IntOverflow)    { exit 93i32; },
        (OutOfBounds)    { exit 94i32; },
        (Unreachable)    { exit 95i32; },
        (WildLeak)       { exit 96i32; },
        (*)              { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
